# Global Coding-Agent Policy: Decision Radar

These instructions apply to all repositories, but only when applicable. Do not create or update decision records for trivial edits, formatting-only work, typo fixes, routine local refactors, generated files, or mechanical dependency metadata changes unless the work exposes a durable decision, contradiction, or drift signal.

## Core Rule

For every meaningful feature, bug fix, behavior change, refactor, integration change, architecture change, operational policy, security/trust-boundary change, user-facing UX rule, AI/LLM behavior change, or production-code change:

Check whether the work creates, changes, confirms, contradicts, or depends on a durable project decision.

## Current Repository Awareness

Before deciding whether a decision record is needed, become aware of the directory/repository the agent is operating in. Prefer repo-local instructions and decision records over global defaults.

At the start of meaningful work, inspect the current repo for relevant context:

- `pwd` / current working directory and repository root
- `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, README, and project docs
- `docs/decisions/INDEX.md`
- `docs/decisions/BACKLOG.md`
- `docs/decisions/REVIEW-CADENCE.md`
- `docs/decisions/ADR-*.md`
- architecture, product, planning, runbook, security, roadmap, or design docs
- implementation and tests when they may contradict documented decisions

If the repo has its own decision system, follow it. If the repo has no decision system, do not create one by default. Create or propose the smallest useful `docs/decisions/` structure only when the current task creates a real durable decision, drift signal, unresolved ambiguity, or the user asks for a decision system.

## Decision Triggers

Look for decision-record opportunities when work involves:

- platform, framework, language, vendor, or dependency choice
- architecture boundary or data ownership choice
- data model, persistence, cache, queue, event, or index strategy
- security, privacy, auth, secrets, admin, custody, wallet, or trust-boundary change
- operational policy: retry, alert, rollout, rollback, backup, incident response, on-call
- frontend behavior: routing, state ownership, data freshness, loading/error states, accessibility rules
- API contract, public interface, compatibility, versioning, or migration policy
- AI/LLM behavior: model choice, prompt contract, tool boundary, eval gate, human review
- user-facing product rule, pricing rule, eligibility rule, safety rail, or compliance-sensitive copy
- reversal, weakening, superseding, or contradiction of a previous decision
- new evidence that changes confidence in an existing decision
- recurring debate that should stop being rediscovered

## Applicability Filter

Create or update a decision record only when at least one is true:

- future contributors would reasonably ask why this choice was made
- changing it later would be costly, risky, or user-visible
- multiple options were plausible and tradeoffs matter
- it affects security, privacy, money movement, data integrity, reliability, operations, or public API/UX contracts
- it resolves an explicit ambiguity or contradicts existing docs/code
- it changes confidence in an existing ADR

Do not create decision records for routine implementation details, local refactors with no durable policy, one-off bug mechanics, formatting, typo fixes, or obvious code-style choices already covered by existing conventions.

## Drift Detection

When touching an area with existing decisions, compare the work against the current repo records. Flag drift when:

- code or tests contradict an ADR
- a plan/spec says something different from implementation
- an ADR review date has passed
- a backlog item has enough evidence to resolve
- a decision lacks owner, status, confidence, sources, review date, or what-would-change-our-mind
- a decision's assumptions no longer hold
- a dependency, vendor, API, regulation, or operational constraint changed

If drift is found, update the relevant ADR/backlog when evidence is clear. If owner input is needed, mark it as `needs-resolution` instead of guessing.

## Standard Files

When a repo uses decision records, prefer this structure:

```text
docs/decisions/
  INDEX.md
  BACKLOG.md
  REVIEW-CADENCE.md
  ADR-0001-example.md
```

Each ADR should include:

- id
- title
- status: `proposed`, `accepted`, `needs-resolution`, or `superseded`
- date
- owner
- area
- confidence: `low`, `medium`, or `high`
- sources
- supersedes / superseded_by
- review_by
- decision
- context
- options considered
- chosen approach
- tradeoffs
- risks
- what would change our mind
- follow-up work
- source notes

## Rules

- Do not invent decisions.
- Mark inference explicitly.
- Cite repo source paths for decisions.
- Preserve prior rationale; append dated update notes instead of erasing history.
- If sources conflict, mark the decision or backlog item as `needs-resolution`.
- Prefer updating an existing ADR over creating duplicates.
- Keep records concise and useful.

## Final Response Checkpoint

For meaningful coding, architecture, product, operations, security, data, AI, or UX tasks, include:

```text
Decision impact:
- ADR created:
- ADR updated:
- Backlog item added:
- Needs owner input:
- No decision impact:
```

For trivial or clearly exempt tasks, a single line is enough:

```text
Decision impact: None; this was a trivial/exempt change.
```
